A recognizer can compare normalized expressions against descriptions of legal instruction forms. A useful normalization retains widths and distinguishes logical shifts from arithmetic ones. Canonicalization catches known identities quickly but can miss equal effects with different symbolic syntax. A checker can instead ask whether some permitted entry state yields different observations. For a finite bit-vector model as above, a proof that no such state exists justifies replacement relative to that model. A counterexample rejects the candidate, and an inconclusive check keeps the baseline. In a window with memory or exceptions, a larger register mask does not substitute for modeling the missing effects.

An implementation can verify the arithmetic identity once per rule and still check the site's live-out condition and feature flag at each application. The proof is about two specific transition summaries, not about all possible instructions named \texttt{AVG8}. Cache a proof against the semantics version and operand widths; do not cache applicability without the conditions under which the proof was obtained. For this rule, the registers other than \texttt{t} can be projected identically in both summaries. A separate test with the feature disabled checks that a true identity cannot bypass the recognizer.

After a successful replacement, a combiner can revisit windows that overlap the new instruction, because their summaries have changed. Restricting this pass to strictly smaller encoded size makes any sequence of accepted rewrites finite on a fixed block. It does not make the result globally smallest: two attractive windows may overlap, and sometimes a useful triple cannot be reached by first combining any pair. Blindell uses this window-size trade-off to explain why adding peephole combination to expansion improves its reach without removing its locality (\cite[chapter~2, section~2.3.2]{blindell2016instruction}). Document the window and the objective before comparing the result to another selector (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-261}).
